May I add my congratulations for the podcast - the first time I've heard
spoken lojban. What a good sound!

I thought the most effective part was the beginning, hearing "natural"
lojban spoken at a normal speed - even though I didn't understand it all it
gave me a good feel of the cadence. I did pick up the point of pronunciatio=
n
(I'm British), but I still thought the language sounded very elegant and
clear - I think that can be resolved by inviting contributions (as you have=
,
epkat).

If I may suggest for the future: I think we could have more conversational
lojban - perhaps a few sentences each time in the form of a dialogue,
presented bilingually. So contributions could be with people speaking as yo=
u
have in the podcast, with snippets of sentences, then repeated in lojban.
Then perhaps to repeat in lojban without the English. We could then go
through the vocab, perhaps without all the details of x1 etc., but
emphasising the gismu themselves (just like in German one would probably
learn a verb first time without the case that it governed - people can lear=
n
the rest in their other studies)

mu'o mi'e djakis

